Consideration of Bid #R-17-11 for furnishing one new Demo Fire Truck and Small
Equipment for the City of Cheyenne Fire & Rescue Dept. (1% Specific Purpose Sales & Use
Tax).  Reporting for Finance Committee, Mr. Valdez stated there was no recommendation on the
item from the Committee.  Mr. Brown moved to approve the bid from Front Range Fire of
```

### Page 4 of 6

```text
Frederick, CO in the amount of $467,821.00, seconded by Mr. Allen.  Chief Jim Martin,
Cheyenne Fire & Rescue Department, provided information on the current fire truck (1998
Pierce Quantum) to be replaced; age of other backup units; other bids received; bid
specifications, and warranty information submitted with bids.  Discussion followed and
included: Specifications and warranty requirements set forth in the bid document; length of
warranty (frame) coverage (50 years) requested in bid specifications; use of the engine being
replaced after removal from front line service; lower bid received relative to meeting bid
specifications; bid document wording regarding consideration of the time of truck delivery;
lowest bid received ($437,432.00) vs. proposed recommended bidder; bidding process, and age,
life expectancy, usage and maintenance costs for current and reserve engines in use.  Throughout
discussion Chief Martin provided information on plans for extending the life of the current fire
truck after retirement from front line service; frame warranty; Fire and Rescue Apparatus
Committee review of bids received, staff members comprising the Committee and the
Committee’s recommendation to acquire a 2011 Pierce Arrow XT “Demo” engine; importance
of delivery date consideration due to maintenance costs incurred for the current truck;
differences in truck models, capabilities, vehicle frame warranty and time of delivery relative to
bid specifications and bids received, and life expectancy and use of current and reserve fleet
engines.  Upon inquiry, Jim Elias, Director of Public Works, stated he was unsure whether any
frame failures on fire engines have occurred, and John Fox, Division Chief of Operations, Fire
and Rescue Department, explained bidders’ “checked-off” boxes indicating areas of bid
specification compliance on forms submitted with their bid, and importance of frame warranty
coverage that doesn’t expire when an engine is retired from the front line and the engine is put to
other fleet use.  Discussion continued on a 20 vs. 50 year frame warranty; type of fire trucks
utilized; proximity of bidders’ dealer service centers to Cheyenne; consideration of delivery time
frame during bid evaluation process; bidding procedures, and concern with lowest bid received
vs. proposed recommended bidder.  During discussion on frame warranty coverage, Duane
Dusett, Front Range Fire, explained the 50 year warranty provided by Front Range’s bid has
been in place since stability frame changes were made to trucks for better stability and
durability.  In response to a procedural question, City Attorney Dan White advised if acceptance
of the recommended bid is rejected, ideally it would be best to reject all bids and re-bid.  It was
confirmed the bid for the Pierce Arrow XT from Front Range Fire was the only vendor that met
all bid specifications.  Mr. White expressed concerns with proceeding with acceptance of a bid
from any other vendor that did not meet specifications. Upon inquiry regarding when it is
decided to place a unit in reserves, Chief Martin advised it is based on maintenance concerns,
expenses, and life of the vehicle, and explained the department’s fleet replacement plan. Upon
inquiry, Mr. Elias indicated any time the City can standardize fleet equipment it may save costs
but each department should determine what type of unit and equipment they need, and Chief
Martin advised bid specifications were developed to be as general as possible and that past bid
documents were used as a guideline.  Chief Martin further discussed warranty time frame;
matching State Land and Investment Board (SLIB) funding deadline connected with the
purchase; recommendation to accept the proposed bid involving a heavier engine, transmission
and frame; different components offered by truck manufacturers, and estimated time frame if re-
bidding of the engine is conducted.  Upon query, Rudy Apodaca, Director of Purchasing, read
bid document wording regarding acceptance of the lowest bid in the sole discretion of the
governing body.  Pursuant to inquiry regarding the time frame for utilizing SLIB funds, Mayor
Kaysen recessed the meeting for 15 minutes to contact the Grants Manager regarding the
deadline.  Mayor Kaysen reconvened the meeting at 7:45 p.m. and advised communication with
the Grants Manager was unable to be made.  Mr. Pierson moved to postpone to Feb. 14, 2011,
seconded by Mr. Spiker.  Mr. Dusett advised the demo truck bid by Front Range Fire has extra
features that weren’t required in bid specifications and if the motion to accept the bid is
postponed, it’s possible that the demo truck could be moved out or sold to someone else and
```

### Page 5 of 6

```text
would no longer be available. Discussion followed on the postponement with regard to receipt of
information involving the SLIB funding time frame.  Mr. Pierson withdrew his motion to
postpone with concurrence by Mr. Spiker as second to the motion. As there were no objections
raised by other member of the governing body, Mayor Kaysen announced the motion to
postpone was being withdrawn.  Dr. Rinne moved to amend the main motion to reflect approval
would be contingent upon the availability of SLIB funds, seconded by Ms. Broyles.  Motion to
amend carried.  Voting “yes” – all members of the governing body present.  Main motion as
amended to approve the bid submitted by Front Range Fire for the Pierce Arrow XT demo truck
in the amount of $467,821.00 carried.  Voting “yes” – all members of the governing body
present with the exception of Mr. Spiker voting “no”.
[CA]
Consideration of Bid #R-16-11 for furnishing two new packer trucks for the Sanitation
